  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe improve efficiency by first checking for a provided parent sequence (height_sequence_1) and using it as an initial best candidate. The idea is to:\n- Maintain the LP-based search method\n- Introduce a progressive scheduling system that allocates timemore effectively across different optimized runs\n- Cite a convergence monitoring mechanism that stops unproductive searches early\n- Implement adaptive resource allocation based on timing and iteration counts\n- Create robust fallback routines to handle failures while preserving progress\n- Ensure the search leverages the provided parent sequence as an initial best-known solution\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nThe objective to minimize is: 2n \u00d7 max(convolve(a(a))) / (sum(a))\u00b2\n\nWe use an LP method to find g that maximizes sum(g) subject to the constraint that convolve(q,g) \u2264 M (where M is max(convolve(q,q))). The initial q is normalized to sum=sqrt(2n). The LP formulation has variables g (sequence coefficients), with constraints A_ub\u00b7g \u2264 b_ub and g\u22650. The matrix A_ub is built based on index positions and scaling.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n- Check parent sequence availability first if provided\n- For each candidate set:\n  - Perform normalization and max-convolution\n  - Build constraint matrix using _build_A(q,k)\n  - Execute LP using linprog with scipysolver\n  - Update candidate based on solution\n- Track best score and time constraints\n- Return the best candidate found by the search\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n- The primary search is a multi-start LP optimization\n- Varied resampling strategies are considered:\n  - Starting directions from parent sequence and uniform distribution\n  - Random initialization attempts\n- Iteration counting ensures progress verification\n\n5. Budget and robustness:\n- Estimated time budget is 1300s; we account for convolution and matrix operations\n- Fail-safe mechanisms include timeout handling with fallback candidates\n- Time management involves per-candidate deadlines\n- Convergence detection stops unproductive searches\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n- Candidates are validated against the evaluator\n- The best candidate is always checked for a finite score\n- Fallback to original parent sequence is used if necessary\n- All outputs must be lists of real numbers",
